1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,850
Sweetheart, in nineteen fifty-five,

2
00:00:02,850 --> 00:00:05,456
Sunday in our house had a smell.

3
00:00:05,636 --> 00:00:07,722
My mother put the pot roast in

4
00:00:07,722 --> 00:00:10,086
the oven before we left for church

5
00:00:10,086 --> 00:00:11,129
at nine thirty,

6
00:00:11,129 --> 00:00:13,215
and when we walked back in the

7
00:00:13,215 --> 00:00:14,119
door at noon,

8
00:00:14,119 --> 00:00:16,691
the whole house smelled like beef and

9
00:00:16,691 --> 00:00:17,873
onions and gravy.

10
00:00:18,053 --> 00:00:20,770
Danny would run straight to the kitchen

11
00:00:20,770 --> 00:00:22,163
in his church shoes.

12
00:00:22,343 --> 00:00:24,631
My father would just stand in the

13
00:00:24,631 --> 00:00:26,642
hall with his hat in his hand

14
00:00:26,642 --> 00:00:27,475
and breathe.

15
00:00:27,824 --> 00:00:30,424
Today, people tell me their pot roast

16
00:00:30,424 --> 00:00:32,390
comes out tough and stringy,

17
00:00:32,390 --> 00:00:34,006
like chewing on a shoe,

18
00:00:34,006 --> 00:00:36,184
and the gravy is thin and gray.

19
00:00:36,364 --> 00:00:38,949
So tonight I'm giving you my mother's

20
00:00:38,949 --> 00:00:40,137
Sunday pot roast.

21
00:00:40,317 --> 00:00:42,832
Fork-tender meat that falls apart,

22
00:00:42,832 --> 00:00:45,790
carrots and potatoes that taste like the

23
00:00:45,790 --> 00:00:48,231
gravy, and a gravy so good you'll

24
00:00:48,231 --> 00:00:49,489
want to drink it.

25
00:00:49,669 --> 00:00:52,555
Every step, and the four mistakes that

26
00:00:52,555 --> 00:00:54,150
make pot roast tough.

27
00:00:54,330 --> 00:00:55,624
And stay with me,

28
00:00:55,624 --> 00:00:58,668
because the last mistake is the opposite

29
00:00:58,668 --> 00:01:00,646
of what most people think.

30
00:01:00,997 --> 00:01:02,080
In the fifties,

31
00:01:02,080 --> 00:01:04,608
pot roast was the smartest dinner a

32
00:01:04,608 --> 00:01:05,908
mother could make.

33
00:01:06,088 --> 00:01:08,525
The cheapest cut of beef in the

34
00:01:08,525 --> 00:01:10,726
butcher case, a chuck roast,

35
00:01:10,726 --> 00:01:14,028
about forty-nine cents a pound in nineteen

36
00:01:14,028 --> 00:01:16,072
fifty-five at our A and P,

37
00:01:16,072 --> 00:01:18,588
cooked low and slow until it was

38
00:01:18,588 --> 00:01:21,575
as tender as the most expensive steak.

39
00:01:21,755 --> 00:01:22,886
My mother said,

40
00:01:22,886 --> 00:01:25,298
there's no such thing as a tough

41
00:01:25,298 --> 00:01:26,202
cut of meat,

42
00:01:26,202 --> 00:01:26,655
honey.

43
00:01:26,835 --> 00:01:28,971
Only an impatient cook.

44
00:01:29,320 --> 00:01:32,014
And it fed us for three days.

45
00:01:32,194 --> 00:01:34,156
Sunday was the roast.

46
00:01:34,336 --> 00:01:37,595
Monday was roast beef sandwiches with the

47
00:01:37,595 --> 00:01:38,469
cold gravy.

48
00:01:38,649 --> 00:01:39,954
Tuesday was hash,

49
00:01:39,954 --> 00:01:42,486
fried in the big skillet with the

50
00:01:42,486 --> 00:01:44,942
last potatoes and an egg on top.

51
00:01:45,122 --> 00:01:47,862
Three dinners from one piece of meat.

52
00:01:48,042 --> 00:01:50,329
That's how a family of four lived

53
00:01:50,329 --> 00:01:52,825
on my father's wages from the plant.

54
00:01:53,176 --> 00:01:55,471
The first pot roast I made for

55
00:01:55,471 --> 00:01:57,078
my husband's parents,

56
00:01:57,078 --> 00:01:58,838
in nineteen sixty-five,

57
00:01:58,838 --> 00:02:01,592
I cooked at four hundred degrees for

58
00:02:01,592 --> 00:02:03,735
an hour and a half because I

59
00:02:03,735 --> 00:02:06,260
was nervous it wouldn't be ready.

60
00:02:06,440 --> 00:02:08,403
It was so tough his father sawed

61
00:02:08,403 --> 00:02:10,121
at it with a steak knife for

62
00:02:10,121 --> 00:02:12,268
five minutes without saying a word.

63
00:02:12,448 --> 00:02:15,534
His mother patted my hand and said,

64
00:02:15,534 --> 00:02:16,944
next time, dear,

65
00:02:16,944 --> 00:02:18,091
give it time.

66
00:02:18,271 --> 00:02:19,768
I never forgot it.

67
00:02:19,948 --> 00:02:21,249
Give it time.

68
00:02:21,599 --> 00:02:23,504
Here's what you need,

69
00:02:23,504 --> 00:02:24,048
honey.

70
00:02:24,228 --> 00:02:25,434
One chuck roast,

71
00:02:25,434 --> 00:02:27,468
about three to four pounds,

72
00:02:27,468 --> 00:02:30,331
with nice white streaks of fat running

73
00:02:30,331 --> 00:02:31,159
through it.

74
00:02:31,339 --> 00:02:33,429
Don't buy a lean roast.

75
00:02:33,609 --> 00:02:36,296
The fat and the connective tissue melt

76
00:02:36,296 --> 00:02:38,347
into the meat while it cooks,

77
00:02:38,347 --> 00:02:40,610
and that's what makes it tender.

78
00:02:40,790 --> 00:02:42,998
A lean roast will be dry no

79
00:02:42,998 --> 00:02:44,552
matter what you do.

80
00:02:44,901 --> 00:02:45,819
Then.

81
00:02:45,999 --> 00:02:48,065
Two tablespoons of oil.

82
00:02:48,245 --> 00:02:51,091
Two teaspoons of salt and a teaspoon

83
00:02:51,091 --> 00:02:52,355
of black pepper.

84
00:02:52,535 --> 00:02:55,159
Two onions, cut in big wedges.

85
00:02:55,339 --> 00:02:57,104
Three cloves of garlic.

86
00:02:57,284 --> 00:02:59,873
Two tablespoons of tomato paste.

87
00:03:00,053 --> 00:03:02,522
One cup of beef broth and one

88
00:03:02,522 --> 00:03:03,629
cup of water.

89
00:03:03,809 --> 00:03:06,148
Two bay leaves and a few sprigs

90
00:03:06,148 --> 00:03:08,638
of thyme, or a teaspoon of dried.

91
00:03:08,818 --> 00:03:10,736
And for the vegetables,

92
00:03:10,736 --> 00:03:12,905
six carrots in big chunks,

93
00:03:12,905 --> 00:03:15,657
and two pounds of small potatoes.

94
00:03:15,837 --> 00:03:17,773
In a minute I'll tell you why

95
00:03:17,773 --> 00:03:19,775
the pot matters as much as the

96
00:03:19,775 --> 00:03:20,109
meat.

97
00:03:20,460 --> 00:03:22,480
And you need a heavy pot with

98
00:03:22,480 --> 00:03:23,316
a tight lid.

99
00:03:23,496 --> 00:03:25,527
A Dutch oven is best.

100
00:03:25,707 --> 00:03:27,970
My mother had a black cast iron

101
00:03:27,970 --> 00:03:30,524
one that belonged to Grandma Pearl,

102
00:03:30,524 --> 00:03:32,859
so heavy I couldn't lift it with

103
00:03:32,859 --> 00:03:34,902
one hand until I was twelve.

104
00:03:35,082 --> 00:03:37,346
Heavy pots hold the heat even,

105
00:03:37,346 --> 00:03:39,837
so the meat cooks slow and steady

106
00:03:39,837 --> 00:03:41,271
and never scorches.

107
00:03:41,620 --> 00:03:42,840
Step one.

108
00:03:43,020 --> 00:03:45,529
Take the roast out of the icebox

109
00:03:45,529 --> 00:03:46,706
an hour before,

110
00:03:46,706 --> 00:03:49,529
and pat it completely dry with paper

111
00:03:49,529 --> 00:03:50,078
towels.

112
00:03:50,258 --> 00:03:53,080
Then salt and pepper it all over,

113
00:03:53,080 --> 00:03:54,020
every side.

114
00:03:54,200 --> 00:03:56,243
A dry roast browns.

115
00:03:56,423 --> 00:03:59,721
A wet roast steams and turns gray.

116
00:03:59,901 --> 00:04:02,614
That's the first difference between a pot

117
00:04:02,614 --> 00:04:05,261
roast that tastes like something and one

118
00:04:05,261 --> 00:04:07,180
that tastes like boiled beef.

119
00:04:07,531 --> 00:04:08,657
Step two.

120
00:04:08,837 --> 00:04:11,484
Heat the oven to three hundred degrees.

121
00:04:11,664 --> 00:04:13,495
Heat the oil in the pot on

122
00:04:13,495 --> 00:04:15,467
the stove until it shimmers,

123
00:04:15,467 --> 00:04:16,946
and lay the roast in.

124
00:04:17,126 --> 00:04:19,085
Now leave it alone,

125
00:04:19,085 --> 00:04:19,704
honey.

126
00:04:19,884 --> 00:04:20,992
Don't poke it,

127
00:04:20,992 --> 00:04:22,101
don't move it.

128
00:04:22,281 --> 00:04:24,365
Let it sit for four or five

129
00:04:24,365 --> 00:04:27,375
minutes until the bottom is deep brown,

130
00:04:27,375 --> 00:04:29,073
almost like chocolate.

131
00:04:29,253 --> 00:04:32,027
Then turn it and brown every side

132
00:04:32,027 --> 00:04:33,119
the same way.

133
00:04:33,299 --> 00:04:34,939
If this is helping you,

134
00:04:34,939 --> 00:04:37,363
honey, tap the like button for me.

135
00:04:37,543 --> 00:04:40,143
It helps Grandma more than you know.

136
00:04:40,494 --> 00:04:42,003
Step three.

137
00:04:42,183 --> 00:04:44,245
Take the roast out and put it

138
00:04:44,245 --> 00:04:45,027
on a plate.

139
00:04:45,207 --> 00:04:47,365
Put the onions in the pot and

140
00:04:47,365 --> 00:04:49,522
cook them about five minutes,

141
00:04:49,522 --> 00:04:52,498
stirring, until they're soft and golden.

142
00:04:52,678 --> 00:04:55,171
Add the garlic and the tomato paste

143
00:04:55,171 --> 00:04:56,951
and stir one more minute.

144
00:04:57,131 --> 00:04:59,533
Then pour in the broth and water,

145
00:04:59,533 --> 00:05:02,080
and scrape up every brown bit stuck

146
00:05:02,080 --> 00:05:04,191
to the bottom of the pot with

147
00:05:04,191 --> 00:05:05,502
your wooden spoon.

148
00:05:05,682 --> 00:05:08,200
Those brown bits are the whole flavor

149
00:05:08,200 --> 00:05:09,153
of your gravy.

150
00:05:09,502 --> 00:05:10,617
Step four.

151
00:05:10,797 --> 00:05:12,369
Put the roast back in,

152
00:05:12,369 --> 00:05:14,512
with any juice from the plate.

153
00:05:14,692 --> 00:05:17,317
The liquid should come about halfway up

154
00:05:17,317 --> 00:05:18,798
the sides of the meat,

155
00:05:18,798 --> 00:05:19,673
not cover it.

156
00:05:19,853 --> 00:05:22,198
Tuck in the bay leaves and thyme.

157
00:05:22,378 --> 00:05:24,372
Put the lid on tight and slide

158
00:05:24,372 --> 00:05:26,233
the whole pot into the oven.

159
00:05:26,413 --> 00:05:28,688
And leave it there for three hours.

160
00:05:29,039 --> 00:05:30,269
Step five.

161
00:05:30,449 --> 00:05:32,140
After about two hours,

162
00:05:32,140 --> 00:05:33,062
take it out,

163
00:05:33,062 --> 00:05:36,060
put the carrots and potatoes around the

164
00:05:36,060 --> 00:05:37,597
roast in the liquid,

165
00:05:37,597 --> 00:05:39,595
and put it back in for one

166
00:05:39,595 --> 00:05:40,364
more hour.

167
00:05:40,544 --> 00:05:42,859
If you put the vegetables in at

168
00:05:42,859 --> 00:05:45,026
the start, they turn to mush.

169
00:05:45,206 --> 00:05:48,209
My mother learned that from Grandma Pearl,

170
00:05:48,209 --> 00:05:50,355
who learned it the hard way in

171
00:05:50,355 --> 00:05:53,573
nineteen twenty-two, the first Sunday she was

172
00:05:53,573 --> 00:05:54,145
married.

173
00:05:54,496 --> 00:05:56,144
How do you know it's done?

174
00:05:56,324 --> 00:05:58,460
Stick a fork in it and twist.

175
00:05:58,640 --> 00:06:00,813
If it pulls apart easily,

176
00:06:00,813 --> 00:06:01,682
it's done.

177
00:06:01,862 --> 00:06:03,342
If it fights you,

178
00:06:03,342 --> 00:06:04,649
it's not ready.

179
00:06:04,829 --> 00:06:06,856
Put the lid back on and give

180
00:06:06,856 --> 00:06:08,521
it thirty more minutes.

181
00:06:08,701 --> 00:06:10,725
That's the whole secret,

182
00:06:10,725 --> 00:06:11,232
honey.

183
00:06:11,412 --> 00:06:14,128
Pot roast isn't done by the clock.

184
00:06:14,308 --> 00:06:16,688
It's done when it falls apart.

185
00:06:16,868 --> 00:06:20,328
But first, a shortcut for busy days.

186
00:06:20,678 --> 00:06:22,870
And if you don't want to heat

187
00:06:22,870 --> 00:06:24,760
the oven for three hours,

188
00:06:24,760 --> 00:06:27,481
sweetheart, a slow cooker works too.

189
00:06:27,661 --> 00:06:30,197
Do the browning on the stove first,

190
00:06:30,197 --> 00:06:31,357
never skip that,

191
00:06:31,357 --> 00:06:34,111
then put everything in the slow cooker

192
00:06:34,111 --> 00:06:35,778
on low for eight hours,

193
00:06:35,778 --> 00:06:38,531
vegetables on the bottom and the roast

194
00:06:38,531 --> 00:06:39,039
on top.

195
00:06:39,219 --> 00:06:41,318
Start it before work and dinner is

196
00:06:41,318 --> 00:06:43,294
ready when you walk in the door.

197
00:06:43,474 --> 00:06:45,665
My mother would have loved one of

198
00:06:45,665 --> 00:06:46,063
those.

199
00:06:46,413 --> 00:06:49,197
Take the roast and vegetables out onto

200
00:06:49,197 --> 00:06:51,689
a warm platter and cover them with

201
00:06:51,689 --> 00:06:52,055
foil.

202
00:06:52,235 --> 00:06:53,733
Now the gravy.

203
00:06:53,913 --> 00:06:56,025
Tilt the pot and spoon off most

204
00:06:56,025 --> 00:06:58,069
of the fat that floats on top.

205
00:06:58,249 --> 00:07:00,474
Then put the pot on the stove

206
00:07:00,474 --> 00:07:01,778
over medium heat.

207
00:07:01,958 --> 00:07:04,858
Shake two tablespoons of flour with half

208
00:07:04,858 --> 00:07:06,597
a cup of cold water in a

209
00:07:06,597 --> 00:07:08,917
jar, and stir it slowly into the

210
00:07:08,917 --> 00:07:10,004
bubbling juice.

211
00:07:10,184 --> 00:07:12,756
Cook for three minutes until it's thick

212
00:07:12,756 --> 00:07:13,481
and glossy.

213
00:07:13,832 --> 00:07:16,414
Taste it, and add salt and pepper

214
00:07:16,414 --> 00:07:17,744
until it's right.

215
00:07:17,924 --> 00:07:19,468
If it's too thick,

216
00:07:19,468 --> 00:07:21,012
a splash of water.

217
00:07:21,192 --> 00:07:22,183
If it's pale,

218
00:07:22,183 --> 00:07:23,555
my mother's trick,

219
00:07:23,555 --> 00:07:26,527
a teaspoon of Worcestershire sauce or a

220
00:07:26,527 --> 00:07:28,890
little splash of strong coffee.

221
00:07:29,070 --> 00:07:31,369
Nobody can tell it's coffee.

222
00:07:31,549 --> 00:07:33,444
It just tastes deeper,

223
00:07:33,444 --> 00:07:35,426
like it cooked all day.

224
00:07:35,606 --> 00:07:36,790
Which it did.

225
00:07:37,140 --> 00:07:38,664
So the four mistakes,

226
00:07:38,664 --> 00:07:39,462
sweetheart.

227
00:07:39,642 --> 00:07:42,522
Mistake one, a lean roast.

228
00:07:42,702 --> 00:07:44,919
Buy chuck, with the fat in it.

229
00:07:45,099 --> 00:07:47,653
Mistake two, skipping the browning.

230
00:07:47,833 --> 00:07:49,854
If you just put the raw roast

231
00:07:49,854 --> 00:07:51,386
in the pot with water,

232
00:07:51,386 --> 00:07:53,685
you get gray meat and gray gravy.

233
00:07:53,865 --> 00:07:56,355
Five minutes a side makes all the

234
00:07:56,355 --> 00:07:57,185
difference.

235
00:07:57,365 --> 00:07:59,603
And the next one is the biggest

236
00:07:59,603 --> 00:08:00,686
mistake of all.

237
00:08:01,036 --> 00:08:03,532
Mistake three, too hot an oven.

238
00:08:03,712 --> 00:08:05,805
People turn it up to three fifty

239
00:08:05,805 --> 00:08:07,636
or four hundred to hurry it.

240
00:08:07,816 --> 00:08:10,844
High heat squeezes the meat tight like

241
00:08:10,844 --> 00:08:13,314
a fist, and it gets stringy and

242
00:08:13,314 --> 00:08:13,632
dry.

243
00:08:13,812 --> 00:08:15,531
Low and slow.

244
00:08:15,711 --> 00:08:17,499
Three hundred degrees.

245
00:08:17,679 --> 00:08:19,080
And mistake four,

246
00:08:19,080 --> 00:08:20,976
taking it out too soon.

247
00:08:21,156 --> 00:08:22,251
If it's tough,

248
00:08:22,251 --> 00:08:23,815
it isn't overcooked.

249
00:08:23,995 --> 00:08:25,225
It's undercooked.

250
00:08:25,405 --> 00:08:26,845
Give it more time.

251
00:08:27,195 --> 00:08:29,389
Slice it thick against the grain,

252
00:08:29,389 --> 00:08:31,384
or just pull it apart with two

253
00:08:31,384 --> 00:08:33,512
forks, and lay it on the platter

254
00:08:33,512 --> 00:08:36,239
with the carrots and potatoes all around.

255
00:08:36,419 --> 00:08:38,727
Pour some gravy over the top and

256
00:08:38,727 --> 00:08:40,819
put the rest in a gravy boat.

257
00:08:40,999 --> 00:08:42,006
In our house,

258
00:08:42,006 --> 00:08:44,253
it came with a basket of soft

259
00:08:44,253 --> 00:08:46,733
rolls and a dish of green beans,

260
00:08:46,733 --> 00:08:49,290
and my father said the blessing a

261
00:08:49,290 --> 00:08:51,227
little longer on Sundays.

262
00:08:51,578 --> 00:08:53,502
And Monday's sandwich,

263
00:08:53,502 --> 00:08:54,027
honey.

264
00:08:54,207 --> 00:08:56,063
Cold roast sliced thin,

265
00:08:56,063 --> 00:08:58,726
on white bread with a little cold

266
00:08:58,726 --> 00:09:00,985
gravy spread on like butter,

267
00:09:00,985 --> 00:09:02,276
salt and pepper.

268
00:09:02,456 --> 00:09:05,511
My father called it the best sandwich

269
00:09:05,511 --> 00:09:06,171
in Ohio.

270
00:09:06,351 --> 00:09:08,158
Or warm it in the gravy and

271
00:09:08,158 --> 00:09:09,765
put it on a toasted bun.

272
00:09:09,945 --> 00:09:12,752
And keep the leftovers in the gravy,

273
00:09:12,752 --> 00:09:14,623
so the meat stays moist.

274
00:09:14,803 --> 00:09:16,602
Four days in the icebox,

275
00:09:16,602 --> 00:09:18,925
or three months in the freezer.

276
00:09:19,275 --> 00:09:21,364
And Tuesday's hash.

277
00:09:21,544 --> 00:09:24,891
Chop the leftover roast and potatoes small,

278
00:09:24,891 --> 00:09:26,525
with a chopped onion.

279
00:09:26,705 --> 00:09:28,529
Fry it all in a hot skillet

280
00:09:28,529 --> 00:09:29,947
with a little butter,

281
00:09:29,947 --> 00:09:31,096
pressing it flat,

282
00:09:31,096 --> 00:09:33,392
and don't stir it for five minutes

283
00:09:33,392 --> 00:09:35,621
so the bottom gets a brown crust.

284
00:09:35,801 --> 00:09:38,350
Turn it, crisp the other side,

285
00:09:38,350 --> 00:09:40,643
and put a fried egg on top.

286
00:09:40,823 --> 00:09:43,346
Danny would eat two plates and ask

287
00:09:43,346 --> 00:09:44,236
for a third.

288
00:09:44,587 --> 00:09:46,180
So one more time,

289
00:09:46,180 --> 00:09:47,210
sweetheart.

290
00:09:47,390 --> 00:09:49,561
A chuck roast with fat in it.

291
00:09:49,741 --> 00:09:50,718
Pat it dry,

292
00:09:50,718 --> 00:09:54,002
salt it, and brown it like chocolate.

293
00:09:54,182 --> 00:09:56,006
Onions, tomato paste,

294
00:09:56,006 --> 00:09:58,176
scrape up the brown bits.

295
00:09:58,356 --> 00:09:59,812
Liquid halfway up,

296
00:09:59,812 --> 00:10:02,968
lid on, three hundred degrees for three

297
00:10:02,968 --> 00:10:03,453
hours.

298
00:10:03,633 --> 00:10:06,268
Vegetables in for the last hour.

299
00:10:06,448 --> 00:10:08,910
It's done when it falls apart.

300
00:10:09,090 --> 00:10:11,918
That's my mother's Sunday pot roast,

301
00:10:11,918 --> 00:10:14,512
and it has never once been tough.

302
00:10:14,862 --> 00:10:17,119
Tell me in the comments,

303
00:10:17,119 --> 00:10:17,683
honey.

304
00:10:17,863 --> 00:10:20,596
What did your family eat after church

305
00:10:20,596 --> 00:10:21,334
on Sunday?

306
00:10:21,514 --> 00:10:23,174
I bet I can guess.

307
00:10:23,354 --> 00:10:26,044
Every day I'm bringing you another recipe

308
00:10:26,044 --> 00:10:27,094
from my kitchen,

309
00:10:27,094 --> 00:10:28,799
the way my mother made it.

310
00:10:28,979 --> 00:10:31,722
And if you want another one tonight,

311
00:10:31,722 --> 00:10:34,921
the whole playlist of Grandma's recipes is

312
00:10:34,921 --> 00:10:36,978
right there on your screen,

313
00:10:36,978 --> 00:10:39,111
and I'll see you in the next

314
00:10:39,111 --> 00:10:39,568
video.

315
00:10:39,748 --> 00:10:41,663
Good night, sweetheart.
